The story of **Esther and Purim** is one of the central survival narratives in Jewish history — a story about escaping genocide in the ancient Persian Empire.

It is told in the biblical Book of Esther and commemorated through the Jewish holiday of Purim.

### The Historical Setting

The story takes place in the Persian Empire under King Ahasuerus (Xerxes I), traditionally identified with Xerxes I, around the 5th century BCE. Many Jews were living in exile throughout Persia after the Babylonian conquest of Judah.

### Main Characters

* Esther — a Jewish woman who becomes queen of Persia.
* Mordecai — Esther’s cousin and guardian.
* Haman — a high-ranking royal official who plots genocide against the Jews.

### The Threat of Genocide

Haman becomes enraged when Mordecai refuses to bow to him. Instead of punishing only Mordecai, Haman persuades the king to issue a decree ordering the destruction of all Jews throughout the empire on a chosen date.

The date is selected by casting lots — called “pur” in Persian — which is where the name “Purim” comes from.

### Esther’s Intervention

Mordecai urges Esther to reveal her Jewish identity and plead with the king, even though approaching the king uninvited could mean death.

Esther famously responds that she will go anyway:

> “If I perish, I perish.”

She invites the king and Haman to banquets, gradually revealing Haman’s plot and exposing him as the architect of the planned massacre.

The king turns against Haman, who is executed on the gallows he had prepared for Mordecai.

### Survival and Reversal

Because Persian royal decrees could not simply be revoked, a second decree allows Jews to defend themselves against attackers. The Jews survive the planned annihilation, and the story ends with celebration and reversal of fortune.

Purim became the annual festival marking this deliverance.

### How Purim Is Celebrated

Today, Purim is celebrated with:

* Reading the Book of Esther aloud
* Booing or making noise when Haman’s name is spoken
* Costumes and festive celebrations
* Giving gifts of food
* Charity to the poor
* Festive meals and drinking

### Themes of the Story

The Esther story is often understood as:

* Survival against attempted extermination
* Courage under political danger
* Hidden identity and revelation
* Reversal of fate
* Minority survival in exile

It has remained especially powerful in Jewish memory because it is one of the earliest narratives centered on the prevention of a mass extermination of Jews in diaspora life.
